<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rai Singh of Bikaner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1541">1541</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rai Singh was raja of Bikaner from 1571 to 1612 and one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> generals of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He built the Junagarh fort at Bikaner between 1589 and 1594, and his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> brought the desert kingdom wealth and high rank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rai-singh-of-bikaner.jpg" alt="Rai Singh of Bikaner" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown_artist_-_Portrait_of_Maharaja_Rai_Singh_of_Bikaner_(reigned_1574%E2%80%931612)_-_2021.11_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rai Singh, a Bikawat Rathore Rajput, was born on 20 July 1541 and took up the rule of Bikaner after his father's death in 1571. In 1576, when the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> was visiting the shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, Rai Singh went there with 6,000 horse to pay him homage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> first as subahdar of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and joined the force sent against the Gujarat Sultanate, a campaign in which the Bikaner forces suffered heavy losses; at its end he received the title of Raja and a grant of 52 parganas. He also took part in the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. In 1585 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khandesh">Khandesh</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and from 1587 to 1592 he was the general in command on the Deccan front. In 1593, when a large force was assembled against Burhan Nizam Shah II, he was principal adviser to Prince Daniyal Mirza, and he was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>. He was also father-in-law of the later emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>.</p><p>While he served on imperial campaigns, the minister Karam Chand Bachhawat ran the affairs of Bikaner. Tension grew between them, and in 1595, after a plot to assassinate Rai Singh was discovered, Karam Chand had to flee. He died in 1604, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> died and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> came to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rai Singh was sent again as governor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>, with his son Sur Singh, and died there on 20 January 1612. Three of his queens and three concubines performed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on his pyre.</p><p>He was among the highest-ranked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nobles of the Mughal court, and his reign is regarded as a turning point in the history of Bikaner. Revenues from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and the spoils of war made the state wealthy, and the capital grew into an opulent town with buildings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junagarh-fort">Junagarh Fort</a>. Idols from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and Sirohi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature paintings</a> he collected were brought to Bikaner, and a rudimentary school of painting that began in his reign evolved into the Bikaner school.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rai Singh of Bikaner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raja <b>Rai Singh</b> served Akbar as a general and built the Junagarh fort.</blockquote><a href="/state/bikaner-state">Bikaner State</a></li></ul></section>
